data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetProperty` value parameter now requires a lambda expression

In EF Core 10, the `SetProperty` overload that accepted a raw value (`TProperty value`) was replaced — the value must now be an `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>`.

Your `Expression.Constant("foo")` passed directly as the value argument will fail.

**Fix:** Wrap the constant in a lambda expression:

```csharp
var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
var valueParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b2");

setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>>(
    Expression.Call(
        instance: setters.Body,
        methodName: nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty),
        typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
        arguments: new Expression[]
        {
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
            // EF Core 10: value must be a lambda, not a bare constant
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Constant("foo"), valueParameter)
        }),
    setters.Parameters);
```

---

## 2. Complex type column naming — disambiguation now required

When multiple complex properties share the same CLR type (e.g., `ShippingAddress` and `BillingAddress` are both `Address`), EF Core 10 no longer silently generates prefixed column names. You must explicitly configure column prefixes to avoid ambiguous `Street`, `City`, etc.

**Fix:** Add explicit column prefix configuration:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street");
        ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_City");
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street");
        ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City");
    });
});

// For nested: Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb =>
    {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ab =>
        {
            ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` gained new members

EF Core 10 added a `CommandSource` parameter to `CommandReaderExecuting` (and the other `Command*Executing`/`Executed` methods).

**Fix:** Update your signature to include the new parameter:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // <-- new in EF Core 10
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

---

### Summary

| Issue | Root cause | One-liner fix |
|-------|-----------|---------------|
| `SetProperty` expression tree | Value param is now `Expression<Func<T,TProp>>` | Wrap constants in a lambda |
| Complex type columns | Ambiguous column names no longer auto-prefixed | Use `HasColumnName` with explicit prefixes |
| Diagnostics logger | New `CommandSource` parameter on interface methods | Add the parameter to your implementation |
